PLACES=[
N('allegheny-county-courthouse','Allegheny County Courthouse and old jail','Pittsburgh','paw','civic','standing',40.43881,-79.99644,'exact','view','walk','','1884–1888',
  'H. H. Richardson\'s courthouse and jail, finished in 1888 and counted among the best Romanesque Revival buildings in the country; Richardson called it his great achievement. It replaced a Greek Revival courthouse of 1836–41 on the same Grant\'s Hill corner. The complex became a National Historic Landmark in 1976. The old jail across Ross Street was converted to court use, keeping its rotunda, cell blocks and warden\'s office.',
  'Two buildings of big rusticated granite blocks with wide arches over the doors and windows, joined across Ross Street by a bridge modelled on the Bridge of Sighs in Venice. Courses of finished stone that were once buried show at the base, where the street in front was lowered in the 1900s. Part of the old cell block holds the Old Allegheny County Jail Museum; its hours were not checked. Interior of the courthouse not checked.',
  'The building Richardson thought his best.',
  '436 Grant Street, downtown, owned by Allegheny County. A working courthouse: look from Grant Street and Ross Street. Downtown has metered street parking and public garages.',
  [W('Allegheny County Courthouse')],brick='no'),
N('union-trust-building-pgh','Union Trust Building (Union Arcade)','Pittsburgh','paw','civic','standing',40.43995,-79.99704,'exact','view','walk','','1915–1916',
  'Henry Clay Frick built it in 1915–16 as the Union Arcade, a shopping arcade of 240 shops and galleries, to a Flemish Gothic design by Frederick J. Osterling (some of the design is credited to Pierre A. Liesch). It stands on the site of the nineteenth-century St. Paul\'s Catholic Cathedral. The Union Trust Company bought it in 1923, renamed it and remodelled the first four floors. It is on the National Register.',
  'A mansard roof with terra cotta dormers and two chapel-like towers that hide the machinery. Inside, the floors are set round a central rotunda under a stained glass dome. Whether visitors may go in to see the rotunda was not checked.',
  'A Gothic shopping arcade that became a bank.',
  '501 Grant Street, downtown, a privately owned office building. Look from Grant Street and Fifth Avenue. Downtown has metered street parking and public garages.',
  [W('Union Trust Building (Pittsburgh)')],brick='?'),
N('pittsburgh-city-county-building','City-County Building','Pittsburgh','paw','civic','standing',40.43811,-79.99686,'exact','view','walk','','1915–1917',
  'The third seat of Pittsburgh\'s government, built in 1915–17 when the city had outgrown the Smithfield Street City Hall of 1868–72, after Allegheny City was joined to Pittsburgh in 1907. It holds mostly city offices, with the county in the buildings next door, and a courtroom used when the Supreme Court of Pennsylvania sits in Pittsburgh.',
  'A downtown office building on Grant Street next to the Richardson courthouse. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  '414 Grant Street, downtown, owned by the city. Look from Grant Street. Downtown has metered street parking and public garages.',
  [W('Pittsburgh City-County Building')],brick='?'),
N('phipps-conservatory','Phipps Conservatory','Pittsburgh','paw','inst','standing',40.43899,-79.94752,'exact','open','walk','','1893–',
  'A Victorian glasshouse in Schenley Park, given to the city in 1893 by the steel and real-estate magnate Henry Phipps Jr. and designed by Lord & Burnham. It grew to a conservatory of fourteen rooms with formal gardens round it, and newer buildings on the grounds are among the most energy-efficient of their kind. It is a City of Pittsburgh historic landmark and on the National Register.',
  'The glass and metal Lord & Burnham conservatory, entered through a modern pavilion. Timed tickets bought ahead; admission charged.',
  'A Victorian glass palace still full of palms.',
  'One Schenley Park, run by a non-profit in the city\'s Schenley Park. Timed tickets are bought ahead; admission from $19.95 for adults on the conservatory\'s own page, more on weekends and evenings. Hours from the conservatory\'s own page (closed Thanksgiving; holiday evening hours from late November). Parking along Schenley Drive in the park.',
  [PHIPPS,W('Phipps Conservatory and Botanical Gardens')],th=[40.43994,-79.94694],thn='Schenley Drive parking, Schenley Park',
  brick='?',hours='Monday to Thursday 9:30 a.m. to 5 p.m., Friday to 10 p.m.; holiday season (late November to early January) daily 9:30 a.m. to 10 p.m.'),
N('allegheny-arsenal-pgh','Allegheny Arsenal (Arsenal Park)','Pittsburgh','paw','fort','traces',40.46663,-79.96114,'site','public','walk','','1814–',
  'A U.S. Army arsenal founded in Lawrenceville in 1814, a supply and manufacturing centre for the Union Army in the Civil War. On September 17, 1862, the day of Antietam, explosions in its cartridge laboratory killed seventy-eight workers, most of them young women: the worst civilian disaster of the war. The cause was never settled. Part of the ground became Arsenal Park in 1907; the main gate was pulled down in 1947.',
  'In Arsenal Park the low stone powder magazine of 1814, the oldest arsenal building left, and a stone wall along 40th Street, with the state historical marker by it. The victims\' monument is in Section 17 of Allegheny Cemetery. In 2012 the Pittsburgh History and Landmarks Foundation warned that the arsenal\'s remaining structures were deteriorating; their state now was not checked.',
  'The forgotten Civil War disaster that happened on the day of Antietam.',
  'A city park bounded by 39th and 40th streets and Butler Street, Lawrenceville, beside Arsenal Middle School. Look at the magazine from the park paths; whether it can be entered was not checked. Street parking nearby.',
  [W('Allegheny Arsenal')],brick='?'),
N('cathedral-of-learning','Cathedral of Learning','Pittsburgh','paw','inst','standing',40.44431,-79.95320,'exact','open','walk','','1926–1937',
  'The 42-storey Late Gothic Revival tower of the University of Pittsburgh, 535 feet tall, commissioned in 1921. Ground was broken in 1926, the first class met in 1931, the outside was finished in 1934 and it was dedicated in 1937. It is a steel frame clad in Indiana limestone and is on the National Register. Its 31 Nationality Rooms were designed by members of Pittsburgh\'s ethnic communities in the styles of their homelands.',
  'Grey limestone Gothic from base to top. Inside, the vaulted, four-storey Gothic Commons Room, ringed on the first and third floors by the Nationality Rooms, which can be seen on guided tours and, on fall weekends, self-guided with a keycard from the visitor centre on the first floor.',
  'A Gothic skyscraper built as a university.',
  '4200 Fifth Avenue, Oakland, on the University of Pittsburgh campus. The Nationality Rooms visitor centre is on the first floor; a step-free entrance is on the side facing Heinz Chapel. Hours from the university\'s Nationality Rooms page (closed Tuesday and on listed holidays; Friday not shown when read). Paid lots and metered street parking round the campus.',
  [NATROOMS,W('Cathedral of Learning')],brick='no',hours='Nationality Rooms: Monday, Wednesday, Thursday, Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., Sunday 11 a.m. to 4 p.m.; closed Tuesday'),
N('heinz-memorial-chapel','Heinz Memorial Chapel','Pittsburgh','paw','worship','standing',40.44526,-79.95186,'exact','open','walk','','1933–1938',
  'A neo-Gothic chapel on the University of Pittsburgh campus, given by the children of H. J. Heinz in memory of him and of his mother, Anna Margaretha Heinz. Charles Klauder, architect of the Cathedral of Learning next door, designed it; ground was broken in 1933 and it was dedicated in 1938, a few weeks after his death. It is a contributing building of the Schenley Farms historic district.',
  'Grey Indiana limestone inside and out, with tall stained glass windows from Charles Connick\'s studio, which also designed much of the carved stonework, including the tympanum over the main door. Open to visitors at set hours.',
  'A Gothic chapel built by the ketchup family.',
  'On the University of Pittsburgh campus beside the Cathedral of Learning, Fifth Avenue and Bellefield Avenue. Visitor hours from the chapel\'s own page; it may close for university holidays and private events. Paid lots and metered street parking round the campus.',
  [HEINZCH,W('Heinz Memorial Chapel')],brick='no',hours='Monday to Thursday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (closed for university holidays and private events)'),
N('allegheny-observatory','Allegheny Observatory','Pittsburgh','paw','inst','standing',40.48249,-80.02080,'exact','open','walk','','1900–1912',
  'An astronomical observatory founded in 1859 by Allegheny industrialists and given to what became the University of Pittsburgh in 1867, where Samuel Pierpont Langley was its first director. The present building in Riverview Park was built in 1900–12. It still uses the 30-inch Thaw refractor, 47 feet long, made by John Brashear. It is on the National Register.',
  'The 1900–12 building in the park, with the Thaw refractor. The public sees inside on guided evening tours, which need a reservation; an open house once a year shows more of the building.',
  'An observatory still using a 1914 telescope.',
  '159 Riverview Avenue, in the city\'s Riverview Park; the building belongs to the University of Pittsburgh. Tours are free and must be reserved; dates are on the observatory\'s events page. Parking on Riverview Avenue by the observatory. The park is open to walk in at other times.',
  [OBS,W('Allegheny Observatory')],th=[40.48231,-80.02106],thn='Riverview Avenue parking, Riverview Park',
  brick='?',hours='Public tours by reservation on set evenings, mostly April to September (see the events page)'),
N('east-liberty-presbyterian','East Liberty Presbyterian Church','Pittsburgh','paw','worship','standing',40.46099,-79.92551,'exact','open','walk','','1931–1935',
  'The fifth church of a congregation founded in 1819 on land given by Jacob and Barbara Negley. Ralph Adams Cram designed it; building began in 1931 and it was finished in 1935, paid for entirely by Richard Beatty Mellon and Jennie King Mellon. It fills a whole city block in East Liberty and is sometimes called the Cathedral of Hope.',
  'A Gothic Revival church with carved stone stained glass by Charles J. Connick, and a large pipe organ rebuilt in 2007 from the original Aeolian-Skinner instrument. Visitors may come to its services.',
  'A cathedral-sized church that fills a city block.',
  '116 South Highland Avenue at Penn Avenue, East Liberty. Service times from the congregation\'s own page. Street parking nearby; parking not checked further.',
  [ELPC,W('East Liberty Presbyterian Church')],brick='?',hours='Sunday worship 9 a.m. and 11 a.m.'),
N('saints-peter-paul-east-liberty','Saints Peter and Paul Church (the "Dogma church")','Pittsburgh','paw','worship','standing',40.46256,-79.91803,'exact','view','walk','','1890–1891',
  'A German Catholic parish church built in 1890–91 to a design by Adolphus Druiding. Lightning set it on fire in 1909 and burned out the inside; it was rebuilt under John T. Comès, with stone columns, a steel and concrete roof and a stone rose window 20 feet across, and rededicated in 1910. It closed in 1992, was sold in 1997 and has stood empty since; in 2018 a court placed it with East Liberty Development, Inc. as conservator. It appears in the last scene of the 1999 film Dogma.',
  'Twin-towered Gothic front with the big stone rose window. Empty and closed: look from the street only.',
  'An empty Gothic church that stood in for a church in Dogma.',
  'On Larimer Avenue, East Liberty. Vacant and closed; look from the public sidewalk only. Street parking nearby.',
  [W('Saints Peter and Paul Church (Pittsburgh)')],brick='?'),
N('saint-anthonys-chapel-troy-hill','Saint Anthony\'s Chapel, Troy Hill','Pittsburgh','paw','worship','standing',40.46492,-79.98376,'exact','open','walk','','1882–1892',
  'A Catholic chapel built by Father Suitbert Mollinger, pastor of Most Holy Name parish, to house the relics of saints he gathered in Europe. He paid for it himself; it was dedicated on June 13, 1883, and enlarged with a nave, rededicated on June 13, 1892, days before he died. His heirs took away chandeliers, an altar and candlesticks; the parishioners bought the chapel and the rest for $30,000. It reopened in 1977 after restoration.',
  'Thousands of relics in cases of crystal, silver and gold line the walls, with life-size linden-wood Stations of the Cross from Mayer of Munich and stained glass windows in the nave.',
  'Thousands of saints\' relics in a hilltop chapel.',
  '1704 Harpster Street, Troy Hill, owned by the Catholic Diocese of Pittsburgh. The chapel\'s own page could not be read when this was written (it refused the request), so hours are not given: other sites disagree on them. Street parking on Troy Hill.',
  [W('Saint Anthony\'s Chapel, Pittsburgh')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('soldiers-sailors-memorial-hall','Soldiers and Sailors Memorial Hall','Pittsburgh','paw','landmark','standing',40.44504,-79.95643,'exact','open','walk','','1907–1910',
  'A Beaux-Arts memorial planned by the Grand Army of the Republic in the 1890s for the county\'s Civil War veterans and built on ground that had been a Union Army mustering field. Henry Hornbostel designed it in 1907 and it was dedicated in 1910. It now honours every branch of the services and is the largest memorial of its kind in the country. It is on the National Register.',
  'A Beaux-Arts hall with an auditorium (its stage backed by the Gettysburg Address), a banquet hall and a military museum.',
  'A Civil War memorial the size of a capitol.',
  '4141 Fifth Avenue, Oakland, run by a non-profit. Its own pages read when this was written gave no museum hours; check them before going. Paid lots round the university.',
  [SSMH,W('Soldiers and Sailors Memorial Hall and Museum')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('allegheny-post-office','Allegheny Post Office (Children\'s Museum)','Pittsburgh','paw','civic','standing',40.45260,-80.00697,'exact','open','walk','','1897–',
  'The main post office of the city of Allegheny, built in 1897, before Allegheny became part of Pittsburgh. It closed in 1967 and was to be pulled down for the Allegheny Center renewal scheme, until the Pittsburgh History and Landmarks Foundation raised about $835,000 to buy and restore it. It reopened in 1972 as the foundation\'s museum and became the first home of the Children\'s Museum of Pittsburgh in 1983. In 1971 it was the first building in Allegheny County on the National Register.',
  'A post office of the 1890s, now the Children\'s Museum, joined to the old Buhl Planetarium building and a newer addition. Admission charged.',
  'A post office saved from the bulldozer by a museum.',
  'Allegheny Square (10 Children\'s Way), North Side. Hours from the museum\'s own page. Parking not checked.',
  [KIDS,W('Allegheny Post Office')],brick='?',hours='Daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('carnegie-library-allegheny','Carnegie Free Library of Allegheny','Pittsburgh','paw','civic','standing',40.45308,-80.00530,'exact','view','walk','','1886–1890',
  'The first Carnegie library commissioned in the United States, given by Andrew Carnegie and built in 1886–90 to a design by John L. Smithmeyer and Paul J. Pelz, of red and grey granite from Vinalhaven, Maine. It opened in 1890, second after Braddock\'s, and held the country\'s first Carnegie Music Hall. After a lightning strike in 2006 the library moved to a new building on Federal Street in 2009.',
  'Red and grey granite walls; the old Carnegie Hall entrance is now the New Hazlett Theater. What the library half is used for now was not checked.',
  'The first Carnegie library ever commissioned.',
  'Allegheny Square East, North Side, beside the Children\'s Museum. Look from the square. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Carnegie Free Library of Allegheny')],brick='no'),
N('ple-station-pittsburgh','Pittsburgh and Lake Erie Railroad Station','Pittsburgh','paw','rail','standing',40.43340,-80.00365,'exact','view','walk','stations','1898–1985',
  'The Pittsburgh and Lake Erie Railroad\'s passenger station, built in 1898 to a design by William George Burns. From 1934 the Baltimore and Ohio\'s trains used it too. It closed in 1985 when the last commuter train ran, and became the Grand Concourse restaurant in Station Square. It is on the National Register.',
  'A Classical Revival station building, now a restaurant inside. Whether the restaurant is open now was not checked.',
  'A railroad waiting room turned dining room.',
  '100 West Station Square Drive, Station Square, South Side, privately owned. Look from the plaza; the inside is a restaurant. Station Square has paid parking lots.',
  [W('Pittsburgh & Lake Erie Railroad Station')],brick='?'),
N('duquesne-incline','Duquesne Incline','Pittsburgh','paw','rail','standing',40.43987,-80.01759,'exact','open','walk','inclines','1877–',
  'A cable railway up Mount Washington, designed by Samuel Diescher and opened on May 20, 1877, at first steam-powered and electric since 1935. It rises 400 feet at 30 degrees along 800 feet of track. When it was to close, Duquesne Heights residents raised money and it reopened on July 1, 1963, run by a non-profit preservation society. It is on the National Register and a Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark.',
  'A Second Empire lower station on West Carson Street, cars restored in the 1970s, and an observation deck at the top over the Golden Triangle. Fares are the regional transit fares.',
  'One of the last two of Pittsburgh\'s seventeen inclines.',
  'Lower station 1197 West Carson Street; owned by Pittsburgh Regional Transit and run by the Society for the Preservation of the Duquesne Heights Incline. Hours from the incline\'s own page; weekend afternoons have the longest lines. Parking near the lower station.',
  [DUQ,W('Duquesne Incline')],brick='?',hours='Every day of the year, 6:30 a.m. to 12:30 a.m.'),
N('monongahela-incline','Monongahela Incline','Pittsburgh','paw','rail','standing',40.43224,-80.00514,'site','open','walk','inclines','1870–',
  'A cable railway up Mount Washington built in 1870 by the Prussian-born engineer John Endres, the oldest continuously working funicular in the United States. It was built when the mills filled the flat land along the river and workers\' homes went up on the hill above. It is on the National Register and a Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark.',
  'The lower station on West Carson Street across from Station Square and the track up the slope. Its cars and stations were not checked.',
  'The oldest working incline in the country.',
  'Lower station on West Carson Street near the Smithfield Street Bridge, across from Station Square and its light-rail station; run by Pittsburgh Regional Transit at transit fares. Hours not checked.',
  [W('Monongahela Incline')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('union-station-pittsburgh','Union Station (Penn Station), Pittsburgh','Pittsburgh','paw','rail','standing',40.44416,-79.99238,'exact','view','walk','stations','1898–1904',
  'The Pennsylvania Railroad\'s station, designed by Daniel Burnham and built in 1898–1904 after the first Union Station burned in the railroad strike of 1877. It served only the Pennsylvania and its lines, so it was renamed Pennsylvania Station in 1912. In the 1980s the office tower became apartments, the Pennsylvanian, and Amtrak moved to an annex on the east side. It is on the National Register.',
  'Burnham\'s domed rotunda over the old carriage drive, in grayish-brown terra cotta that looks like brownstone, and brick. The rotunda is closed to cars, which are too heavy for its brick roadway over the garage below. Amtrak trains still stop here.',
  'Burnham\'s rotunda for carriages, still standing.',
  '1100 Liberty Avenue at Grant Street, downtown. The tower is private apartments and the station part belongs to Amtrak; look at the rotunda from Liberty Avenue and Grant Street. Whether the public may walk under it was not checked. Paid parking at the station.',
  [W('Union Station (Pittsburgh)')],brick='?'),
N('carnegie-institute-library','Carnegie Institute and Library','Pittsburgh','paw','civic','standing',40.44371,-79.94910,'site','open','walk','','1895–',
  'Andrew Carnegie\'s gift to Pittsburgh: the main Carnegie Library, which opened in 1895, with a music hall and the museums of art and natural history under one roof in Oakland. The words "Free to the People" are cut over the library entrance. The complex was listed on the National Register in 1979.',
  'The library, the Carnegie Music Hall with its gilded foyer, and the two museums. Admission charged for the museums.',
  'Carnegie\'s library, music hall and museums in one building.',
  '4400 Forbes Avenue, Oakland. Museum hours from their own page; the library keeps its own hours, not checked. Paid parking in the Carnegie Institute garage on South Neville Street.',
  [CMNH,W('Carnegie Museums of Pittsburgh'),W('Carnegie Library of Pittsburgh')],brick='?',hours='Museums: Wednesday to Monday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Tuesday'),
]
